# We Shall Do What Is Possible and Leave What Is Not

*(Summary of an interview given to journalists in Chennai on 24 February 1967, before assuming office.)*

## Leadership

Why should I conceal it from you? I shall resign as Member of Parliament for South Chennai and assume leadership of the ministry that is to be formed here in Tamil Nadu.

**Question:** Will you leave the South Chennai parliamentary seat vacated by your resignation to a Congress candidate?

**Answer:** That is why some of us say the Central Government should be a national government. Why do you not advise the Government accordingly? Even so, if Congress representatives approach me about the South Chennai parliamentary seat, I shall consider it.

## The Ministry

The newly elected DMK legislators will meet in Chennai next Sunday, 26 February 1967. It may nevertheless take two weeks to form the ministry. It may be larger than the present one, because, as you know, all of us are new to this responsibility. I do not wish to burden ministers by assigning many departments to each. The meeting on Sunday will decide the ministry's size and responsibilities. There will certainly be a woman minister. There will be no deputy ministers assisting the Chief Minister, only one parliamentary secretary.

**Question:** Is there a possibility that M. P. Sivagnanam may become Speaker of the Legislative Assembly?

**Answer:** My friend M. P. Sivagnanam is a capable and excellent speaker. He is needed in the Assembly. I do not wish to silence him by making him Speaker. Besides, he may wish to preserve the separate identity of his Tamil Arasu Kazhagam.

## Clean Administration

The DMK will strive to establish clean and efficient administration.

## The Hindi Question

The DMK will stand firmly by its policy on Hindi and oppose Hindi becoming the sole official language. English must remain the link language until all fourteen national languages become official languages. Restraining the speed with which Hindi is imposed as the official language and amending the Constitution accordingly are among the party's programmes.

## Dravida

A zonal council already exists. We shall examine why authority should not be entrusted to that council instead of leaving it as a merely advisory body. I believe a committee should be constituted to study how the Indian Constitution has functioned during the past twenty years and to identify ways of granting greater powers to the states.

## Authority

States need greater authority in fields such as education, industry, and taxation. They should possess more power to grant industrial licences.

We are not opposed to permits and licences. We condemn the irregularity and corruption that wind through that system.

In my view, it is enough for the Central Government to possess only the authority necessary to protect the integrity and frontiers of the country. Remaining a puppet of the Centre does not constitute economic integrity. Planning must be decentralised and begin from below.

The Constitution should include a mechanism for periodically evaluating how it functions.

I am not demanding Dravida Nadu. I ask only that the states receive greater powers.

Instead of planning for prestige, we shall adopt plans that provide immediate benefit.

## Prohibition

We support prohibition and shall try to implement it more effectively.

## Horse Racing

Horse racing will be abolished completely.

## Nationalisation of Bus Transport

Serious attention will be given to nationalising bus transport. We have already pressed the Congress Government on this matter.

## Land Reform

The DMK ministry will also attend to land reform. Concessions granted under the land-ceiling law will be removed.

## The Food Situation

Our economy is founded upon food commodities. A fall in the price of rice affects other goods too.

We must create in farmers a desire to increase production. The prices of materials needed for agriculture must therefore be reduced substantially. The DMK Government will act vigorously in this direction.

## The Student Question

The student question is not new. It exists everywhere and always. I do not say students should neither understand politics nor participate in it. Indeed, on certain issues they may give us a fresh perspective. But I say they should not stake their education to take part in political agitation.

## Free Education

Education should be provided free to the greatest extent possible, but it must also be of good quality. I shall consult educational experts on this.

## Useful Measures

To learn which problems the Congress Government has left behind, we must first understand the existing situation. The party will then attend to the food problem.

Attention will be given to economy in administration, the widespread establishment of industries in rural areas, and preventing wealth from accumulating in a single place.

Rather than locking up large amounts of capital in schemes executed for empty display without producing good results, priority must be given to projects offering immediate benefit. State plans must therefore be reshaped accordingly. At the same time, the DMK Government will press the Centre to implement already approved projects such as the Salem Steel Plant, the Kalpakkam Atomic Power Station, and the Thoothukudi harbour project, and to take action to reduce the prices of essential goods used in people's daily lives.

## Thanks

The people have decided that the DMK, as the leading party of the alliance, should be given the opportunity to assume responsibility for government. They firmly believe the party has matured enough to accept that responsibility and will therefore do good for them.

Because we have received, in this work, the counsel of experienced political thinkers such as Rajaji and Quaid-e-Millath Muhammad Ismail, the people have complete confidence that the DMK will establish a stable government. I therefore thank those leaders at this time.

*(Summary of an interview given to journalists in Chennai on 24 February 1967, before assuming office.)*
